        passage: "Akio and Lilja are teenagers in two very different countries, and where they live changes their ordinary days. Akio, who lives in Japan, gets up at six. He makes his bed and puts on his uniform. Then he sits down with his family for a typical Japanese breakfast of rice, miso soup and grilled fish. Carrying a packed lunch box, he catches the train to school. Lilja, who lives in Finland, wakes up at seven. She showers, puts on casual clothes and eats yoghurt or bread and cheese before she cycles off to school. In Japan, schools demand discipline and high standards. After lessons, the students clean their classrooms, then go to clubs such as judo, calligraphy or robotics. Akio chose robotics, where he helps to build and code robots. Later he goes to a cram school for extra lessons, to be ready for the university entrance exams. He finishes the day with his homework and some manga. In Finland, lessons are built around teamwork and creative thinking. At break time Lilja heads out to talk with her friends and breathe some fresh air. Only some Finnish schools have clubs, offering things like music, art or sports. Lilja belongs to a photography club, where she takes pictures of nature. At home she does an hour of homework, helps with the chores and practises her violin. Nature documentaries are a favourite with her family, who often watch them together. Akio's day is full of study and rules, whereas Lilja has more room for creativity, fresh air and family time.",
